# Intro
Uniform, metal-enriched gas in a periodic box that expands with the universe, cooling
radiatively with Grackle or geajay. The gas starts at 100 K, well above the temperature of the
cosmic microwave background (CMB), T_CMB(z) = T_CMB,0 (1 + z). With the CMB temperature floor of the
cooling library on, the gas cools onto T_CMB(z) and stays there. With the floor off, it cools
far below it. The box holds 32768 gas particles at 10 H atoms/cm^3 and a metal mass fraction
of 0.01, with no gravity and no stars, and expands by a factor 1.25 in scale factor.

The floor is an option of the cooling library. In official Grackle it is
`GrackleCooling:cmb_temperature_floor` and T_CMB,0 is 2.73 K. In geajay it is
`GeajayCooling:metal_cooling_cmb_floor` and T_CMB,0 is 2.725 K. Both default to 1. They subtract the
metal cooling rate at T_CMB(z) from the metal cooling rate, so metal cooling stops at
T_CMB(z). `agora_cmb_temperature_floor` is a different option: SWIFT applies it itself as a
lower bound on the internal energy, and this example leaves it at 0.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_1 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
Use `grackle_1` or a higher mode. With geajay, replace `--with-cooling=grackle_1` and
`--with-grackle=...` by `--with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay`.

# Run
```
redshift=9 cmb_floor=1 ./run.sh
redshift=9 cmb_floor=0 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_z<redshift>_floor<cmb_floor>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an
environment variable: `n_threads`, `redshift` (starting redshift), `cmb_floor`
(1 or 0), `gas_density` (atom/cm^3, physical), `gas_mass` (Msun), `temperature` (K),
`expansion` (a_end / a_begin), `n_steps`, `n_snapshots`, `swift_exe`, `run_dir` and
`extra_args` (further swift arguments). The metal mass fraction is in `params.yml`.

# Check
```
python3 checkCMBFloor.py run_z9_floor1 --t-cmb-0 2.73
```
Use `--t-cmb-0 2.725` for geajay. The snapshots hold no temperature, so the script rebuilds it
from the internal energy, the species and the metal mass fraction, as the cooling library does.
In every snapshot after the first it checks that all temperatures are finite and that the lowest
one is at least T_CMB(z) within 1e-4 plus half the time step in ln a (floor held). In the last
snapshot it checks that the highest one is within 1e-3 above T_CMB(z) (floor reached). It exits
with status 1 if a check fails. A run with `cmb_floor=0` is expected to fail the floor-held check:

```
python3 checkCMBFloor.py run_z9_floor0 --t-cmb-0 2.73 --expect-below-floor
```
exits with status 0 only if that run fails the floor-held check and all its temperatures end
below half of T_CMB(z). A leg takes a few minutes on 4 threads with the default 400 steps. The
result depends on the time step of the run: raise `n_steps` to check that it has converged.
